## 1. My Intuition

This problem is similar to Minimum Genetic Mutation (433).

In Genetic Mutation, we generate possible mutations using four characters: A, C, G, T.

Here, instead of four characters, we have all 26 lowercase English alphabets.

We need to find the shortest transformation sequence from beginWord to endWord, where each transformation changes exactly one character and the resulting word must exist in the dictionary.

## 2. My Approach

1. Store all words from wordList in a HashSet for efficient lookup.
2. If endWord is not present in the set, return 0.
3. Start BFS from beginWord.
4. For every word:
   - Iterate through each character position.
   - Try replacing that character with all 26 lowercase English letters.
   - If the generated word exists in the set, add it to the queue and remove it from the set.
5. Process BFS level by level.
6. Return the word count when endWord is reached.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

No major difficulty in the BFS logic.

The main observation was recognizing that this problem follows the same approach as Minimum Genetic Mutation, with a larger character set.

## 4. What Clicked

The transformation of a word can be treated as generating its neighboring nodes.

Two words are connected if they differ by exactly one character.

BFS guarantees that the first time we reach endWord, we have found the shortest transformation sequence.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int ladderLength(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>(wordList);

        if (!set.contains(endWord)) {
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<String> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(beginWord);
        set.remove(beginWord);

        int count = 1;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                String word = q.poll();

                if (word.equals(endWord)) {
                    return count;
                }

                for (int j = 0; j < word.length(); j++) {
                    for (char ch = 'a'; ch <= 'z'; ch++) {
                        if (word.charAt(j) == ch) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(word);
                        sb.setCharAt(j, ch);

                        String newWord = sb.toString();

                        if (set.contains(newWord)) {
                            q.offer(newWord);
                            set.remove(newWord);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            count++;
        }

        return 0;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

Let N be the number of words and L be the length of each word.

- **Time Complexity:** O(26 × N × L²)
- **Space Complexity:** O(N × L)

Each word is processed at most once. For each word, we generate up to 26 × L possible transformations, and creating each string takes O(L).

## 7. Revision Notes

- Single-source BFS.
- Generate neighbors by changing one character at a time.
- Use HashSet for dictionary lookup.
- Remove words from the set when adding them to the queue to avoid revisiting.
- BFS level count represents the number of words in the transformation sequence.
